# one body per type and one call per value, at three levels of nesting. the allocation assertions are the
# half that says it is *correct* - five buffers built, five given back - and the RAST is the half that says
# it is one body rather than a copy per drop site
--- OUT --->
2
2
1
balanced
--- RAST --->
# three locals, three calls, reverse declaration order at the scope end
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Inner&>(varref<Inner>(var($c))))
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Outer&>(varref<Outer>(var($b))))
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Outer&>(varref<Outer>(var($a))))

# `Inner`'s body reaches its own private property and nothing else
CHECK: function $deinit -> type<void>
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<array<int32>&>(ma<array<int32>>(varref<Inner&>(var($this))->numbers)))

# and `Outer`'s reaches one level down only - two calls, in reverse declaration order, naming `Inner`'s
# teardown rather than repeating what it does. `$numbers` is not spellable from here and does not appear
CHECK: function $deinit -> type<void>
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Inner&>(ma<Inner>(varref<Outer&>(var($this))->second)))
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Inner&>(ma<Inner>(varref<Outer&>(var($this))->first)))
